-- EXPECT: case needs its clauses written right before it
1 1 1 case

-- EXPECT: 'case' takes more values than the stack holds here.
{'ok (drop 1) 'no (drop 0)} case

-- EXPECT: case clauses come in pairs, a key and a body
1 'ok tag {'ok} case

-- EXPECT: case cannot take this value: it may be tagged 'b, which is not one of the tags this takes.
-- a case names every tag its value may carry
0 (5 'a tag) (6 'b tag) if {'a (drop 1)} case drop

-- EXPECT: the value may be tagged 'no, which no clause names
-- a result case names both 'ok and 'no, or ends in '_
5 ok {'ok (drop 1)} case drop

-- EXPECT: 'f' takes
-- EXPECT: it may be tagged 'c, which is not one of the tags this takes
-- a case without '_ closes its input's tags, so a caller's other tag is refused at the call
( {'a (drop 1) 'b (drop 2)} case ) 'f let  5 'c tag f drop

-- EXPECT: a case clause key is a 'tag or '_, not this. Branch on other values with if.
5 {(3 lt) (drop 1)} case drop

-- EXPECT: '_ is the key of case's catch-all clause, so it cannot be a tag.
5 '_ tag drop

-- EXPECT: the '_ clause takes every tag the others do not name, so it comes last.
5 'a tag {'_ (drop 0) 'a (drop 1)} case drop

-- EXPECT: this case has two clauses for 'a.
5 'a tag {'a (drop 0) 'a (drop 1)} case drop

-- EXPECT: case needs a clause for at least one tag.
5 'a tag {} case drop

-- EXPECT: case cannot take this value: this value is never tagged 'b.
-- the outer case closes x's tags to 'a, so the inner 'b clause is dead
1 'a tag 'x let x {'a (x {'a (print) 'b (print)} case)} case

-- EXPECT: the clauses name every tag the value may carry, so the '_ clause never runs. Delete it.
( 5 'ca tag ) [{'ca int} either move out] effect 'g let  g {'ca (drop 1) '_ (drop 2)} case drop

-- EXPECT: the clauses name every tag the value may carry, so the '_ clause never runs. Delete it.
-- a result carries only 'ok and 'no
5 ok {'ok (drop 1) 'no (drop 2) '_ (drop 3)} case drop

-- EXPECT: a result is tagged only 'ok or 'no, so this case cannot also name 'a. Match 'a in another case, or use '_.
1 ok {'ok (drop 1) 'a (drop 2)} case drop

-- EXPECT: the '_ clause gets the tagged value, tagged 'b
-- '_ takes the value whole, so a box payload cannot be dropped there
5 box 'b tag {'a (drop 1) '_ (drop 0)} case drop

-- EXPECT: 'f' takes
-- EXPECT: this value is never tagged 'b
-- a closed set meets a word that takes more tags
( {'b (drop 2) 'a (drop 1)} case ) 'f let  ( 5 'a tag ) [{'a int} either move out] effect 'g let  g f drop

